1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,060
Listen to a lecture in an Earth Science class.

2
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:08,120
Today I’d like to explain the Mohs’ scale, used in what is called the “scratch test.”

3
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:13,110
This scale is based on the simple fact that harder minerals scratch softer ones.

4
00:00:13,110 --> 00:00:14,741
For example, a diamond;

5
00:00:14,741 --> 00:00:17,620
a quartz crystal can scratch a feldspar crystal,

6
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:19,170
but not the other around.

7
00:00:19,170 --> 00:00:25,100
The scale is named for Friedrich Mohs, the mineralogist who devised it in 1812.

8
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:31,000
His scale spans the range of minerals known at that time, from the softest to the hardest.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,996
By performing a scratch test using known minerals and a few common tools, an

10
00:00:35,996 --> 00:00:40,150
unidentified mineral sample can be placed between two points on the scale.

11
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:44,120
By referring to the scale, the mineral can then be identified.

12
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:48,824
I have here a collection of the minerals included on the Mohs’ scale, as

13
00:00:48,824 --> 00:00:52,000
well as the tools necessary to complete this exercise.

14
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,563
I’d like you each to take a mineral sample from the basket at the front of the room

15
00:00:56,563 --> 00:01:00,020
and classify it according to its place on the Mohs’ scale.

16
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:03,050
First, however, I should give you a little warning.

17
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:07,010
The hardness of any mineral depends on the strength of the bonds between ions

18
00:01:07,010 --> 00:01:11,110
or between atoms – the stronger the bond, the harder the mineral.

19
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:15,945
Because bond strength may differ in various angles of a crystal, the hardness may

20
00:01:15,945 --> 00:01:19,917
vary slightly depending on the direction in which the mineral sample is scratched,

21
00:01:19,917 --> 00:01:24,090
so be sure to scratch each sample in several different directions.
